In this document is a guild to run a ROS2 package. Follow the instructions to successfully upload the package onto the TurtleBot and run the code.
The mission of this TurtleBot is to navigate the environment while avoiding every possible obstacle in its way. Moreover, the Bot should only run for a minute. The ROS2 code is designed to use two topics on the Turltebot, the first being the /Cmd_vel and the second is the /scan topic. These topics use messages of type twist and laserscan respectively.

Follow the steps below to get the Turtlebot4 up and running.
1)	Downloading the package from the lab code repository through the link provided. https://labcode.mdx.ac.uk/Mahmoud_Alabadla/my_package.git. 
2)	Open 3 terminals on your virtual machine 
a.	In terminal one, type the command (ssh ubuntu@192.168.8.ID) to access the TurtleBot’s Raspberry Pi and then run the command line (ros2 launch rplidar_ros rplidar_c1.launch) to activate the lidar and keep it running.
b.	In terminal two, right the command line (scp -r my_package ubuntu@19.168.8.ID:my_package) to copy the package into the robot’s root folder.
c.	In a third terminal, re-enter the command (ssh ubuntu@192.168.8.ID) to enter the robot's Rasberry Pi once more.
3)	Using the terminal, move the package from the root folder on the workspace and then into the source folder using the following command. (mv my_package ros_ws/src/)
4)	Now enter into the workspace by typing the command line, (cd ros_ws/)
5)	Here, we can build the package and its dependencies by typing the command, (Colcon Build).
6)	Followed by a command line (Source install/setup.bash).
7)	Lastly, to run the code, type the line (ros2 run my_package my_node). 

On the terminal, a print loop will print the remaining time left for the robot to shut down which is a one-minute timer after which the screen should display “mission accomplished”.

